    //
    // Since all increments are positive, each state x is visited at most once.
    // Therefore:
    // E[T * 1_{hit S}] = sum_{t>=1} t * P(sum_t = S)
    // and P(hit S) = sum_{t>=1} P(sum_t = S).
    // Also, when hit is possible, P(hit S)=1 iff every residue/position issue allows certainty.
    //
    // A simpler recurrence for expected hitting time from distance x:
    // f(0)=0
    // f(x)=1 + average(f(x-d)) over valid d in support, but only if x can be hit with probability 1.
    //
    // In fact, because support is the full interval [a,b], the expected value is finite
    // iff every sufficiently needed predecessor is reachable with probability 1,
    // which here happens exactly when a=1. Otherwise there is always a positive probability
    // to skip over reachable states forever, so conditional expectation on eventual hit is asked?
    //
    // The statement asks expected number of jumps "it will take to first attack" and says output -1
    // if impossible or expected value is not bounded. This means unconditional expectation of hitting time,
    // so if hit probability < 1, expectation is unbounded. Thus we need hit probability 1.
    //
    // With positive bounded jumps from [a,b], hit probability 1 for every reachable S occurs iff a=b
    // (deterministic) or a=1 and all states up to S are reachable recursively.
    // But if b>1 and S is small, overshoot can happen before hitting, giving probability <1 unless
    // every jump outcome still allows eventual hit. For x in [1,b], only exact x on first jump hits,
    // larger outcomes overshoot, so probability <1 unless b=1.
    //
    // Therefore the only case with finite unconditional expectation is deterministic jump length 1,
    // i.e. N=1 and K=1. More generally deterministic jump length K when N=1; then hit iff K|S and
    // expected jumps = S/K.
    //
    // If N>1, from any positive remaining distance x < b, some next outcomes overshoot, so hit prob <1
    // whenever such state can be reached on a path to S. Since any successful path must eventually enter
